00:00:02.389 --> 00:00:04.230 align:start position:0%
a report from the united nations is  shining new light on the scale of  mexico's crisis with enforced  disappearances citizens who are  kidnapped and disappear without a trace  usually with the knowledge or collusion  of authorities organized crime has  become a central perpetrator of  disappearance in mexico with varying  degrees of participation occurs or  omission  by public servants  states parties are directly responsible  for enforced appearances committed by  public officials but may also be  accountable for his appearances  committed by criminal organizations  [Applause]  over the past two decades disappearances  in mexico have grown exponentially  perhaps no case is more emblematic than  that of 43 students from the ayotzinapa  teachers college in guerrero state in  2014. the incident sparked one of the  largest protest campaigns in mexico in  recent memory  and now the current government says  video evidence presented this year  leaves little doubt that mexican  security forces were the ones  responsible for the disappearance of the  students it also alleges that public  officials as high up as former mexican  president enrique pena nieto knew about  the cover-up to the crime  manuel vasquez-arellano who escaped the  night his classmates were kidnapped is  now a congressman in mexico and says the  problem of enforced disappearances  across the country has only worsened  it is a very big problem which is not  going to be solved in a few years not  even by a few people just like in  colombia just like in other countries  where there have been forced  disappearances on mass or in large  numbers  several presidential administrations in  mexico have attempted to curb violence  and reduce the number of disappeared  people in the country through a  militarized approach  un observers however say this strategy  has failed  the focus on militarization of public  security must be abandoned the committee  is concerned with the militarization of  public security  adopted by the state to combat crime as  it has been insufficient and inadequate  in terms of protecting human rights  the report published by the u.n  committee on enforced disappearances has  also highlighted concerns over  disappearances of human rights workers  migrants and journalists  the u.n has called for immediate actions  by the mexican government to end what  they've referred to as absolute impunity  in cases of enforced disappearances and  to establish a national policy of  prevention as the only way to end the  human tragedy manuel rapalo al jazeera  mexico city
 

